<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Castelfidardo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Castelfidardo was fought on 18 September 1860 near Ancona between the army of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> under Enrico Cialdini and the papal army under the French general Lamoricière. The Sardinian victory allowed the occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, which voted to join the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>, leaving the pope with only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-castelfidardo.jpg" alt="Battle of Castelfidardo" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: it:Giovanni Gallucci (b. 1815 - d.: unknown) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Gallucci_La_Battaglia_di_Castelfidardo_palazzo_comunale_di_Castelfidardo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 7 September 1860 the Sardinian prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Camillo Benso, Count of Cavour</a>, sent an ultimatum to the Papal Secretary of State, Cardinal Giacomo Antonelli, demanding the dismissal of the foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> units in the papal army. When it was refused, the Sardinian army crossed the border on 11 September. General Louis Juchault de Lamoricière aimed to march his field army to the port of Ancona, fortify it and await help by sea from the Catholic powers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manfredo-fanti">Manfredo Fanti</a>, in overall command, and Enrico Cialdini sought to intercept him first.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The papal field army of about 10,000 men was largely made up of foreign volunteers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and Ireland. By 17 September Cialdini's IV Corps blocked the inland road to Ancona, and Lamoricière, outnumbered and outgunned, sent General Georges de Pimodan with the vanguard to hold the Sardinians in the valley of the Musone while the main body slipped along the coast past Monte Conero. On 18 September de Pimodan attacked and took several fortified farmhouses, but Sardinian artillery and infantry retook them.</p><p>Lamoricière turned back to save his vanguard, giving up his march to Ancona, and arrived to find the papal lines overwhelmed. De Pimodan was mortally wounded, and the survivors fled over the Conero promontory. The Sardinians lost 61 killed and 184 wounded; the papal army 88 killed, about 400 wounded and around 600 captured.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The papal field army ceased to exist as a fighting force. Lamoricière and the remnants reached Ancona, which surrendered on 29 September after a siege. Plebiscites in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> in November 1860 approved their annexation, which joined the northern and southern lands held by the Sardinian crown and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> and prepared the proclamation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in March 1861. An ossuary built in 1861 holds the dead of both armies, and a national monument by Vito Pardo was unveiled on Monte Cucco in 1912.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Castelfidardo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Garibaldi handed southern Italy to Victor Emmanuel, whose troops had crossed the Papal States after defeating the papal army at <b>Castelfidardo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century the small papal army, reinforced by foreign volunteers including the papal Zouaves, was defeated by Piedmont at <b>Castelfidardo</b> in 1860 and held Rome only with French help, winning at Mentana in 1867.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li></ul></section>
